  <section class="wrap fiche-body">
    <div>
      <h2>The vibe</h2>
      <p>The city of Hyères created this dog beach on Mérou beach, Boulevard du Front de Mer, after a petition with 25,000 signatures — the area has existed since August 2022. Unlike many beaches that close to dogs in summer, this one stays open year-round, with no seasonal restriction. The area is marked off with wooden "ganivelle" fencing and clear signage, and a few pines provide some welcome shade on hot days.</p>

      <h2>What to know before you go</h2>
      <p>The ground is a mix of sand and pebbles depending on the spot. A free, partially shaded municipal car park sits about 200 m past the village of L'Ayguade, on the right coming from Hyères — it fills up fast on weekends in high season, so it's best to arrive early. Poop-bag dispensers and bins are installed at the entrances to the area. The exact leash/off-leash rule could not be confirmed with absolute certainty from an official source consulted remotely: as a precaution, keep your dog on leash on arrival and watch the on-site signage.</p>
      <div class="callout">Coco's tip: the <a href="/en/fiche-balade-giens">Giens peninsula</a> and its landscapes between two seas are worth exploring right nearby if you're extending your stay in the area — avoid July-August in the middle of the day if you want to use the car park without stress.</div>

      <h2>Nearby</h2>
      <p>The <a href="/en/fiche-le-pradet-caniplouf">Cani Plouf in Le Pradet</a>, to the west, is another good year-round option in this part of the Var. In Hyères itself, a <a href="/en/fiche-toiletteur-nalla">groomer</a> and the <a href="/en/fiche-restaurant-o-creol">O'Créol restaurant</a> near Port de la Capte let you round out the day without leaving the area.</p>
